{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":385102,"duration_api_ms":377101,"num_turns":48,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"8a36b371-fe42-4fb2-9de8-937a802d106e","total_cost_usd":2.2252534999999996,"usage":{"input_tokens":264,"cache_creation_input_tokens":85010,"cache_read_input_tokens":2585492,"output_tokens":15995,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":85010,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":264,"outputTokens":15995,"cacheReadInputTokens":2585492,"cacheCreationInputTokens":85010,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":2.2252534999999996,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"summary":"Thorough review of the budget helpers implementation (tanksim-6e5.1.2.2). The core deliverables are well-implemented: `step_and_inspect` provides ergonomic test stepping with budget inspection, the assertion API (`assert_n_conserved`, `assert_c_conserved`, `assert_o2_balanced`, `assert_budget_balanced`, `assert_per_tick_balanced`) enables single-call conservation checks, debug output via `TANK_BUDGET_DEBUG=1` works correctly, and per-system delta inspection is fully functional. The budget types (`BudgetLedger`, `TickBudgetRecord`, `ElementBudget`, `BudgetDelta`) are cleanly designed with proper snapshot vs explicit recording distinction. The engine integration correctly gates budget overhead behind `Option<BudgetLedger>` so disabled tracking has zero runtime cost. All 22 budget-specific tests pass. The env var synchronization for parallel tests uses a mutex + thread-local re-entrancy guard, which is correct. The `debug_assert` in `record_stage` ensures explicit deltas can't diverge from actual state changes. The existing test failures (tracing.rs compilation error and assertion failure) are from other beads (6e5.3.7, 6e5.1.2.4, 6e5.4.2), not from this bead's changes. No critical bugs or safety issues found.","amendments":[{"body":"The `carbon_guard_delta_mg` function in engine.rs:828 exempts the entire `system:chemistry` stage from the carbon budget guard by subtracting its full net delta. While a `debug_assert` in `record_stage` validates explicit-delta consistency in debug/test builds, this check is compiled out in release builds. If budget tracking is ever enabled in release mode (e.g., for production diagnostics), a carbon-conservation bug inside the chemistry system would be silently masked by the guard. Consider promoting the `delta_net_matches_totals` check from `debug_assert!` to a proper runtime check (returning `SimError`) when budget tracking is active, or at minimum document this release-build gap in the budget guard's doc comment.","rationale":"The `debug_assert` in `record_stage` (budget.rs:176) is the only thing preventing the chemistry exemption from masking carbon bugs. In release builds, this assert is elided, leaving the guard blind to chemistry-internal carbon leaks. Since budget tracking can be enabled at runtime without recompilation, there's a real scenario where this matters."},{"body":"The `with_budget_debug_env` function (budget_helpers.rs:151-163) uses `std::env::set_var` and `std::env::remove_var`, which are `unsafe fn` starting from Rust edition 2024. The project currently uses edition 2021 (Cargo.toml:4), so this compiles today, but the calls will become compilation errors when the crate migrates to edition 2024. Consider wrapping these in `unsafe` blocks now with a SAFETY comment explaining the mutex synchronization, or switching to an internal `AtomicBool` / `OnceLock<bool>` flag instead of reading an actual environment variable for the debug toggle, which would avoid the env-var mutation entirely.","rationale":"Rust edition 2024 makes `set_var`/`remove_var` unsafe because they mutate process-global state that can race with other threads. While the existing Mutex provides adequate synchronization for the budget-debug use case, migrating to edition 2024 will force this change anyway. An internal atomic flag would be simpler and avoid the thread-safety ceremony entirely."},{"body":"The `budget_helpers` module is `pub mod` in lib.rs:1, making all its public items (`step_and_inspect`, `assert_n_conserved`, `Element`, `BudgetInspector`, etc.) part of tank_core's public API. These are test/debug utilities that downstream crates should not depend on for production logic. Consider gating the module behind `#[cfg(any(test, feature = \"budget-debug\"))]` or documenting it as `#[doc(hidden)]` to signal it is not a stable API surface.","rationale":"If another crate depends on `tank_core::budget_helpers` for production logic, the test-oriented API becomes a stability commitment. Gating behind a feature flag or cfg(test) would make the intent clearer and prevent accidental coupling."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"d3542274-282e-42ce-943a-cb1e4e6184c5"}
